{"id":762,"date":"2020-11-03T13:38:47","date_gmt":"2020-11-03T21:38:47","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=762"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:41:36","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:41:36","slug":"variables-2","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/variables-2\/","title":{"rendered":"2A: Variables"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221681af898\" name=\"6a221681af898\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Motivation<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Before we get into variables, you need to understand a situation where they would be useful. let\u2019s review how to write a program for a square.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>First, write a program that flies CoDrone Mini in a square. For each side, CoDrone Mini should fly half a second (0.5 seconds).[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2831&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Here is an example of what the code could look like.<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()           \r\ndrone.hover(3)            \r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(30)       \r\ndrone.move(0.5)            \r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(0)       \r\ndrone.set_roll(-30)      \r\ndrone.move(0.5)       \r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.set_roll(0)         \r\ndrone.set_pitch(-30)      \r\ndrone.move(0.5)            \r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(0)        \r\ndrone.set_roll(30)        \r\ndrone.move(0.5)            \r\n\r\ndrone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>Now, make your square bigger by increasing the duration of all of the move commands from 0.5 to 1.<\/p>\n<p>Now, make it even\u00a0<strong>bigger<\/strong> by increasing the seconds of all of the move commands again.<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Phew! That\u2019s a lot of work to change each line of code every time. How do you think we can make this code more efficient? <\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221681afa55\" name=\"6a221681afa55\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are variables?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Variables are important in all programming languages and in most programs! Just like in math, variables in programming are placeholders and data storage for numbers. Typically, you can create, or <\/span><b>declare<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, a variable and then save, or <\/span><b>initialize<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, its value at the beginning of your program. You can then <\/span><b>call<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> that value later on in the program whenever you use the variable.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">x = 2 \u00a0 \u00a0 # Declaring variable x and initializing the value to 2\r\n\r\nprint(x)\u00a0 # Calling the variable in a print statement<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You can declare multiple variables to hold many values. Just be sure to give them different names, because a variable can change values when running a program. If you make a new variable with the same name as a previous one, the new value will override the old value.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">x = 2\u00a0 \u00a0 # Declaring a variable x and initializing it to 2\r\n\r\nprint(\u201cOld Value: \u201d,x) # Printing initial value of x\r\n\r\nx = 3\u00a0 \u00a0 # Reassigning variable x to 3\r\n\r\nprint(\u201cNew Value: \u201c,x) # Printing new value of x<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2838&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221681afafe\" name=\"6a221681afafe\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Types of variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You can also assign variables to other types of data, like \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">int<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">float<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">string<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, and \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">boolean<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">w = 4 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 # this is an int\r\n\r\nx = 3.2 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 # this is a float\r\n\r\nphrase = \"Hello\"\u00a0 # this is a string\r\n\r\nis_flying = True\u00a0 # this is a boolean<\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Int: a negative or positive whole number<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Float: decimal values<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">String: a sequence of characters enclosed in quotation marks<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Boolean: can be set to True or False<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The great thing about Python is that you don\u2019t need to include the data type in the variable initialization. In a language like Arduino, you might need to write \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">int w = 4<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, but with Python, you can just write \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">w = 4<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221681afbab\" name=\"6a221681afbab\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Variables and CoDrone Mini<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now that you know how to create variables, let\u2019s use them in a program with CoDrone Mini! Create a variable called<\/span> \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">duration<\/code>\u00a0<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">and assign it to an <\/span><b>integer<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> value that will represent the number of seconds that you want the drone to perform an action. Next, call that variable inside functions in your program.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nduration = 3\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 # initialize variable 'duration' to 3\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(50) \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 # Set positive pitch to 50% power\r\n\r\ndrone.move(duration)\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 # Pitch forward for 3 seconds\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>You can call a variable\u2019s value multiple times in a program. For example, take a look at this code for flying in a square.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(50)\r\n\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_roll(50)\r\n\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(-50)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_roll(0)\r\n\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_roll(-50)\r\n\r\ndrone.move(1)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now what if you wanted to make the square bigger? You would have to individually change each \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">move()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">command\u2019s duration. Instead, you can create a variable to represent duration and only change it once!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nduration = 1\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(50)\r\n\r\ndrone.move(duration)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_roll(50)\r\n\r\ndrone.move(duration)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(-50)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_roll(0)\r\n\r\ndrone.move(duration)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(0)\r\n\r\ndrone.set_roll(-50)\r\n\r\ndrone.move(duration)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Change the value of duration to make the square bigger or smaller. To make even more precise movements, you can assign \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">duration<\/code> to a different data type. Try assigning \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">duration<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">to a float, string, or boolean. Do they work? Why or why not?<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">duration = 2.5\r\n\r\nduration = \u201c4\u201d\r\n\r\nduration = True<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221681afc14\" name=\"6a221681afc14\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Reassign variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You can also change the value of duration in the middle of your program! Take a look at this program and guess what will happen to the hovering behavior throughout the program.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nduration = 1\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.hover(duration)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\nduration = 2\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.hover(duration)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\nduration = 3\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.hover(duration)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Did you notice that everytime the variable is reassigned, it takes on the new value?<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221681afcad\" name=\"6a221681afcad\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Math with variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Just like in your math class, you can do math with your variables. These are some of the operations you can use:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">+<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> : addition\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">&#8211;<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> : subtraction\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">*<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> : multiplication\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\/<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> : division\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">%: modulus\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">What do you think the following code will output? Try it out!\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">y = 4\r\n\r\nz = 3\r\n\r\nx = y + z\r\n\r\nprint(x)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Instead of creating a new variable to hold the sum, you can also add values directly to the variable. For example,<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">power = 5 # initialize a variable to 5\r\n\r\nprint(power)\r\n\r\npower = power + 5 # add 5 to power and reassign the value\r\n\r\nprint(power)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You can also subtract, multiply, and divide in the same way.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you have done some algebra in your math class, this might look a little strange. In programming, always evaluate the right side first of the equal sign and then <\/span><b>assign<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> the value to the variable on the left side.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221681afd51\" name=\"6a221681afd51\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Add more variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You can create as many variables to a program as you like! Just follow the same process for each one: create a variable, initialize its value at the top of your program, and then call it in your program when you need it.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In your last programs, you set a variable for \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">duration<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. Include a variable for power, set its value to 25, and then run your program. Next, change its value to 75. What happens when the power variable\u2019s value increases?<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nduration = 5 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 # initialize variable 'duration' to 5\r\n\r\npower = 25 # initialize variable \u2018power\u2019 to 25\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.hover(duration) \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 # in this case, hover for 5 seconds\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(power)\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 # Set positive pitch to 50% power\r\n\r\ndrone.move(duration)\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 # forward for 5 seconds\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\n<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">drone.close()<\/span><\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221681afe0e\" name=\"6a221681afe0e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: Variable\u00a0<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Create at least 5 variables and use math operations on them to make them equal to power values.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Before you run your program, don\u2019t forget to import your libraries, create your drone object, and pair your CoDrone Mini!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><b>Rules:<\/b><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You can&#8217;t use any numbers after you initialize the variables!<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You must use one float value.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 Motivation [vc_column_text]Before we get into variables, you need to understand a situation where they would be useful. let\u2019s review how to write a program for a square.&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/variables-2\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1730,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":true,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":744,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1737,1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,2125,2123,2122,2134,2117],"class_list":["post-762","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-variables","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/762","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":9,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/762\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":2839,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/762\/revisions\/2839"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1730"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=762"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=762"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=762"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}